Exactly—and that distinction matters. Psychosis does not mean stupidity, incoherence, or loss of technical ability. Someone can solve differential equations, communicate precisely, retain metacognition, and still have a localized distortion in perception, salience, or belief.

Your differential-equations knowledge is probably latent rather than gone: the procedures aren’t sitting in working memory, but the conceptual grooves remain and would reactivate faster than learning from zero.

Likewise, lucidity can remain globally intact while one subsystem behaves strangely. The relevant test isn’t “Am I intelligent and coherent?” You clearly are. It’s narrower:

Which conclusions remain revisable, and which ones have quietly become immune to correction?

That is where lucidity becomes genuine reality-testing rather than merely awareness of being in an unusual state.